WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.820
Meiosis gives us the diversity that we
see in our everyday lives. It's a

2
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.900
special type of cell division found in
eukaryotes that sexually reproduce,

3
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.850
including plants, fungi and animals
like us.

4
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.160
Let's take human meiosis as an example,
a process that gives rise to our

5
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:18.360
gametes,

6
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:19.950
eggs and sperm.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.470
Human gametes have one copy of each of
the 23 chromosomes, which is half the

8
00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.050
number of most of our body cells.

9
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:32.920
For simplicity, we will only follow two
chromosomes through meiosis. When the

10
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.910
egg and spoon fuse, they produce a
fertilized cell, which has 46

11
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.080
chromosomes, one copy from your mother
and one from your father.

12
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.460
So how do gametes end up with only half
the number of chromosomes? That's where

13
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.650
meiosis comes in. So how does meiosis
work? Meiosis starts with a cell that

14
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:55.840
contains two copies of every chromosome.
Each copy of the chromosome is

15
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.300
replicated to double the number of
chromosomes.

16
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.050
Each identical copy is called a
chromatid.

17
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.900
The two identical purple chromatids
pair up with the two identical yellow

18
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.390
chromatids, and this is where swapping
of DNA occurs, in a crossing over

19
00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.350
process called recombination.

20
00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.450
It's the first step in which genetic
diversity is introduced.

21
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.540
After crossing over, the pairs of
chromatids line up along the center of

22
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:23.050
the cell.

23
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.260
The pairs are then pulled to opposite
ends of the cell by a protein machine

24
00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:33.220
called the spindle. This is the second
step where diversity is introduced, as

25
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.860
it is random whether the mother or the
father pair of chromatids goes to one

26
00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.360
end of the cell or the other.
Immediately following this first

27
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.360
division, there is a second division
without any DNA replication. This

28
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.270
division reduces the number of each
chromosome from two, diploid, to one,

29
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:50.850
haploid. Thus,

30
00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.340
when the gametes, the sperm and the
egg, fuse together, we restore the

31
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.050
normal number of chromosomes for the
next generation.

32
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.430
Can you now see how meiosis gives us
the diversity that we see in our

33
00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:07.250
everyday lives? It's the reason you
don't look identical to your mom or

34
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.150
your dad. Without meiosis, we would all
be clones of each other.

